    {
      "questionId": "q0",
      "value": "q0_pay_when_roi_clear",
      "rationale": "As a busy parent juggling school and activities, I'd want to test the free tier first to confirm it actually reduces my stress and missed deadlines. Once I see clear ROI in saved time and fewer scrambles, I'd upgrade to Plus.",
      "confidence": 0.88
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q1",
      "value": "q1_plus_after_sustained_use",
      "rationale": "Pro's email parsing is nice but not essential—I can manually add school events. After a month or two of sustained use with Plus, I'd know if the extra $5 for automation is worth it.",
      "confidence": 0.82
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q2",
      "value": "q2_monthly_cancel_anytime",
      "rationale": "I prefer flexibility. Monthly billing lets me cancel guilt-free if life gets less hectic or the app doesn't deliver. Prepaying annually feels like a big commitment for an unproven product.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q3",
      "value": "q3_one_dollar_try_cancel",
      "rationale": "A dollar is low-friction enough to test Plus features properly. I'd use it for a month, see if handoffs and the digest actually save time, then decide whether to pay full price or drop back to free.",
      "confidence": 0.87
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q4",
      "value": "q4_compare_pay_if_wins",
      "rationale": "I'm not reflexively loyal to paid apps, but I also won't waste time hunting for inferior free alternatives. If FocusLoop clearly outperforms what my friend uses, I'll pay. Otherwise, I'll stick with what works.",
      "confidence": 0.83
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q5",
      "value": "q5_ads_pay_if_plus_useful",
      "rationale": "Ads are mildly annoying but tolerable on free. I'd upgrade to Plus for the handoffs and digest, not to remove ads. Ad-free alone wouldn't justify the cost.",
      "confidence": 0.84
    },
    {
      "questionId": "q6",
      "value": "q6_fair_if_use_justifies",
      "rationale": "Six to twelve dollars per month is reasonable for a family coordination tool, but only if I use it daily and it genuinely cuts down stress and forgotten appointments. If it sits unused, it feels overpriced.",
      "confidence": 0.86
    },
    {
      "questionId": "overall_interest",
      "value": 4,
      "rationale": "The product directly addresses my pain point—scattered school and activity info—and the free tier is accessible. I'm excited to try it, though I need proof it actually reduces my mental load before committing to paid.",
      "confidence": 0.87
    },
    {
      "questionId": "would_try_beta",
      "value": "false",
      "rationale": "Absolutely. As a dual-income parent with a school-age child, I'm exactly the target user. A beta would let me test whether the weekly digest and handoffs truly simplify our household coordination.",
